A streaming CSV parser must handle quoted newlines without loading the whole file. This is the core difficulty: a naive line-based reader breaks the moment a quoted field contains `\n`, which is legal in RFC 4180 CSV. The parser therefore cannot assume "line = record"; it must scan character by character (or chunk by chunk) and track whether it is inside a quoted field.

Below is a complete implementation. It reads the file in fixed-size chunks (e.g., 64 KiB), maintains a small state machine, and yields records one at a time. Memory usage is bounded by the chunk size plus one record, regardless of file size.

